1
00:00:00,128 --> 00:00:03,607
So we have this project here, this image

2
00:00:04,121 --> 00:00:06,317
optimization project which we worked on.

3
00:00:06,927 --> 00:00:09,300
Now, thus far, I always

4
00:00:09,893 --> 00:00:10,968
used this tool

5
00:00:11,497 --> 00:00:13,774
by running this optimize images script, or

6
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:15,490
we could run this command here, of course,

7
00:00:15,506 --> 00:00:17,478
directly in the command line. And there is

8
00:00:17,574 --> 00:00:20,429
nothing wrong with this, but Bun has another

9
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:23,058
nice feature which can be very useful if you

10
00:00:23,187 --> 00:00:23,796
wanted to

11
00:00:24,389 --> 00:00:27,035
distribute a tool like this to other people.

12
00:00:27,452 --> 00:00:29,571
Because right now, if I wanted to share this

13
00:00:29,683 --> 00:00:31,144
tool with someone else,

14
00:00:31,706 --> 00:00:34,081
that someone else needs to install Bun on

15
00:00:34,113 --> 00:00:36,200
their system, right? Because we're using the

16
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,302
Bun runtime, we're using the Bun tool here.

17
00:00:38,543 --> 00:00:41,143
So Bun must be installed on the machine

18
00:00:41,175 --> 00:00:43,133
where you wanna run this program.

19
00:00:43,823 --> 00:00:45,798
And if I wanna share that with a friend who

20
00:00:45,814 --> 00:00:48,205
doesn't even know about Bun, that of course

21
00:00:48,301 --> 00:00:49,008
could be a bit annoying.

22
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:51,720
And that's where another nice feature of Bun

23
00:00:51,848 --> 00:00:55,093
comes into play. The single file executable

24
00:00:55,302 --> 00:00:55,623
feature.

25
00:00:56,186 --> 00:00:57,326
Bun allows you

26
00:00:57,953 --> 00:00:59,351
to compile

27
00:00:59,946 --> 00:01:03,015
any TypeScript or JavaScript code into a

28
00:01:03,015 --> 00:01:05,746
standalone executable, into a single file

29
00:01:05,746 --> 00:01:07,980
that can be shared with others, that can be

30
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:10,550
executed on any system, Windows, Linux,

31
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:11,016
macOS,

32
00:01:11,595 --> 00:01:13,877
without Bun needing to be installed.

33
00:01:14,503 --> 00:01:16,335
And it's this command which we can execute

34
00:01:16,335 --> 00:01:18,633
to produce such a single file executable.

35
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:21,776
Obviously, we must plug in our file name

36
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,736
here. So the root entry point of our

37
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,149
program, in my case, optimize-images.ts,

38
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:30,355
and then --compile compiles that to a single

39
00:01:30,355 --> 00:01:31,314
file executable.

40
00:01:33,038 --> 00:01:35,083
So here I'll name this opt-image.

41
00:01:36,081 --> 00:01:37,305
That will be the name of the

42
00:01:38,513 --> 00:01:40,767
executable of the tool that will be created.

43
00:01:41,322 --> 00:01:42,155
And if I run this,

44
00:01:42,809 --> 00:01:45,211
it'll go ahead and now I have this opt-image

45
00:01:45,554 --> 00:01:46,028
thing here.

46
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:48,762
And that's now a binary file that can be

47
00:01:48,842 --> 00:01:49,789
executed like this.

48
00:01:50,767 --> 00:01:52,965
On Windows, it should have had.exe

49
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:54,120
extension already.

50
00:01:54,938 --> 00:01:57,376
And that then is this entire tool bundled

51
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,200
into one executable. And that's of course a

52
00:02:00,248 --> 00:02:03,793
super useful feature for distributing code.

53
00:02:04,242 --> 00:02:06,825
Now, it's worth noting that you can also set

54
00:02:06,825 --> 00:02:10,065
specific targets. By default, it'll use your

55
00:02:10,274 --> 00:02:12,439
operating system as a target. So in my case,

56
00:02:12,503 --> 00:02:13,033
macOS.

57
00:02:13,646 --> 00:02:15,430
If I knew that I wanted to build for

58
00:02:15,512 --> 00:02:18,755
Windows, I could add --target and then for

59
00:02:18,755 --> 00:02:19,907
Windows x64,

60
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:23,282
bun-windows-x64, for example.

61
00:02:23,845 --> 00:02:25,762
Or if you know that you're only targeting

62
00:02:25,859 --> 00:02:27,469
recent versions of Windows, you could use

63
00:02:27,469 --> 00:02:28,339
the modern version.

64
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:30,902
So I'll set my target to bun-windows-x64.

65
00:02:32,786 --> 00:02:34,664
That'll go ahead and do its thing.

66
00:02:36,587 --> 00:02:37,588
And now I got that

67
00:02:38,201 --> 00:02:40,219
.exe file here, which I could share with

68
00:02:40,235 --> 00:02:42,220
Windows users. And you can do the same thing

69
00:02:42,495 --> 00:02:43,205
for other operating systems too, of course.

70
00:02:45,214 --> 00:02:47,837
And that therefore is of course an extremely

71
00:02:48,014 --> 00:02:50,057
valuable feature when building utility

72
00:02:50,057 --> 00:02:52,374
tools, CLI tools like the one we have here,

73
00:02:52,615 --> 00:02:54,835
because that makes sharing them so much

74
00:02:55,189 --> 00:02:55,527
easier.
